1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,030
Imagine a country at peace.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,400
Factories make cars and refrigerators.

3
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,470
Farmers grow food.

4
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:09,530
Companies build houses.

5
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,770
People go to work, earn money,
and spend it on things they want.

6
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:15,954
And then a war begins.

7
00:00:16,984 --> 00:00:22,094
Suddenly the government needs millions
of uniforms. Trucks. Artillery shells.

8
00:00:22,264 --> 00:00:24,334
Aircraft, ships and tanks.

9
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:28,821
But there is a problem. Where
do all these things come from?

10
00:00:29,577 --> 00:00:31,977
You cannot simply print
a trillion dollars and

11
00:00:32,057 --> 00:00:34,807
magically create a trillion
dollars' worth of tanks.

12
00:00:35,646 --> 00:00:40,656
A war economy is, fundamentally, a giant
redistribution of a country's resources.

13
00:00:40,966 --> 00:00:44,536
And that creates some deeply
counterintuitive problems.

14
00:00:47,326 --> 00:00:51,096
In a normal economy, production
is driven by what civilians want.

15
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:54,876
People want houses, so
companies build houses.

16
00:00:55,808 --> 00:00:58,328
During a major war, the
government becomes one

17
00:00:58,408 --> 00:01:01,458
of the largest customers
in the entire economy.

18
00:01:02,197 --> 00:01:05,077
Instead of asking what
consumers want, the economy

19
00:01:05,157 --> 00:01:07,887
increasingly asks what
the war effort needs.

20
00:01:08,757 --> 00:01:12,967
A factory that used to build cars
might start building military trucks.

21
00:01:13,877 --> 00:01:18,147
A steel mill that supplied construction
companies might be supplying armour plate.

22
00:01:18,946 --> 00:01:21,476
The machinery hasn't necessarily changed.

23
00:01:22,266 --> 00:01:24,396
The destination of the resources has.

24
00:01:25,295 --> 00:01:27,345
But where do the resources come from?

25
00:01:28,721 --> 00:01:30,631
A country has only so much steel.

26
00:01:31,101 --> 00:01:33,291
So much copper, oil and electricity.

27
00:01:33,841 --> 00:01:35,611
So much factory capacity.

28
00:01:35,741 --> 00:01:37,441
So many skilled workers.

29
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:39,051
And so much time.

30
00:01:39,831 --> 00:01:41,851
If the military suddenly
needs much more of

31
00:01:41,931 --> 00:01:44,661
all of that, somebody
else has to get less.

32
00:01:45,495 --> 00:01:48,165
Suppose the government
spends billions on weapons.

33
00:01:49,195 --> 00:01:51,045
The money itself isn't the weapon.

34
00:01:51,823 --> 00:01:54,663
The real question is what
happens to the steel, the

35
00:01:54,743 --> 00:01:57,433
workers and the machines
that build those weapons.

36
00:01:58,361 --> 00:02:00,421
If those resources were
making civilian goods

37
00:02:00,501 --> 00:02:03,651
before, then civilian
output has to shrink...

38
00:02:04,601 --> 00:02:07,891
unless the country can expand
what it is able to make in total.

39
00:02:08,706 --> 00:02:11,596
So a war economy is not
about spending more money.

40
00:02:12,346 --> 00:02:16,516
It is about moving real resources from
one part of the economy to another.

41
00:02:19,206 --> 00:02:20,406
And then there is labour.

42
00:02:21,606 --> 00:02:24,216
A modern war can require
millions of soldiers.

43
00:02:24,346 --> 00:02:28,636
But soldiers don't make cars,
build houses, or run factories.

44
00:02:29,597 --> 00:02:34,607
So the economy faces a rather strange
problem. The war needs workers.

45
00:02:35,537 --> 00:02:36,927
And the war also takes workers away.

46
00:02:38,444 --> 00:02:41,074
Governments respond in various ways.

47
00:02:41,704 --> 00:02:44,494
Women enter industries
that were dominated by men.

48
00:02:45,184 --> 00:02:46,894
Older workers stay employed.

49
00:02:47,644 --> 00:02:49,074
Working hours go up.

50
00:02:51,284 --> 00:02:54,114
Britain is a particularly
interesting example.

51
00:02:54,964 --> 00:02:59,354
When the Second World War ended in
nineteen forty-five, Britain had won.

52
00:03:00,116 --> 00:03:05,006
But victory did not mean the country was
suddenly rich again. Quite the opposite.

53
00:03:05,995 --> 00:03:09,525
Years of total war had
consumed enormous resources.

54
00:03:10,475 --> 00:03:13,135
Britain had accumulated
large debts, and its

55
00:03:13,215 --> 00:03:15,505
industry was still
committed to the war effort.

56
00:03:16,355 --> 00:03:19,485
The war was over. But rationing continued.

57
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:23,405
People still needed ration
books for many basic goods.

58
00:03:23,695 --> 00:03:25,745
And not just for a few months.

59
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:30,577
Rationing in Britain finally
ended in nineteen fifty-four.

60
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:33,250
Almost nine years after the war it fought.

61
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:37,088
Which illustrates an important principle.

62
00:03:37,958 --> 00:03:41,688
A country can win a war and still
come out of it economically exhausted.

63
00:03:42,669 --> 00:03:46,479
Military victory and economic
prosperity are not the same thing.

64
00:03:49,379 --> 00:03:51,769
Now we get to the most confusing part.

65
00:03:52,611 --> 00:03:54,741
Imagine a factory builds a tank.

66
00:03:55,531 --> 00:03:58,501
That production counts towards
gross domestic product.

67
00:03:59,451 --> 00:04:03,141
The tank is sent to the
battlefield. It gets destroyed.

68
00:04:04,135 --> 00:04:06,125
The government orders another tank.

69
00:04:06,795 --> 00:04:08,345
More production is recorded.

70
00:04:09,289 --> 00:04:10,939
So production is happening.

71
00:04:11,649 --> 00:04:13,519
But has society become richer?

72
00:04:14,401 --> 00:04:17,291
Gross domestic product
counts the second tank.

73
00:04:18,041 --> 00:04:19,911
It never subtracts the first one.

74
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:22,111
The measure isn't useless.

75
00:04:22,981 --> 00:04:24,671
It measures economic production.

76
00:04:25,641 --> 00:04:29,191
But production is not the same thing
as wealth, or as living standards.

77
00:04:30,151 --> 00:04:32,521
And that gap can grow surprisingly wide.

78
00:04:35,211 --> 00:04:38,301
A war can create something
that looks exactly like a boom.

79
00:04:39,231 --> 00:04:43,341
Factories run at full
capacity. Unemployment falls.

80
00:04:44,571 --> 00:04:45,941
Industrial output explodes.

81
00:04:46,897 --> 00:04:50,947
So someone looks at all that and
says: war stimulates the economy.

82
00:04:51,878 --> 00:04:54,908
But there is a question hiding
underneath that sentence.

83
00:04:55,638 --> 00:04:57,348
Stimulates it to do what?

84
00:04:58,141 --> 00:05:00,391
The economy has been mobilised.

85
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:04,591
It has not discovered a magical
way to create unlimited resources.

86
00:05:07,561 --> 00:05:10,411
So how does a government
actually pay for all of this?

87
00:05:11,177 --> 00:05:13,807
Taxes. Borrowing. War bonds.

88
00:05:13,977 --> 00:05:17,707
And, in some circumstances,
creating additional money.

89
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:20,031
But there is no free lunch.

90
00:05:20,841 --> 00:05:23,101
If a government tries to
use more resources than the

91
00:05:23,181 --> 00:05:26,571
economy can physically
provide, something has to give.

92
00:05:27,354 --> 00:05:30,704
Imagine there are a hundred units
of civilian goods available.

93
00:05:31,154 --> 00:05:34,324
But people suddenly have much
more money in their pockets.

94
00:05:35,345 --> 00:05:39,555
The factories have been redirected, so
there are still only a hundred units.

95
00:05:40,476 --> 00:05:44,686
More money chasing the same amount
of goods means prices tend to rise.

96
00:05:45,564 --> 00:05:49,154
Which is why war economies so
often end up rationing things.

97
00:05:50,012 --> 00:05:52,952
Rationing is basically
the government saying: you

98
00:05:53,032 --> 00:05:55,862
have money, but you cannot
buy as much as you want.

99
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:57,900
Why?

100
00:05:58,575 --> 00:06:00,765
Because money is not the scarce resource.

101
00:06:01,435 --> 00:06:03,045
The physical goods are.

102
00:06:03,515 --> 00:06:06,325
There may be enough money
for everyone to buy petrol.

103
00:06:07,075 --> 00:06:08,865
There simply isn't enough petrol.

104
00:06:09,651 --> 00:06:12,501
So instead of letting the
price rise without limit,

105
00:06:12,751 --> 00:06:15,721
the government limits how
much each person can buy.

106
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:18,631
And whenever legal supply
is restricted while

107
00:06:18,711 --> 00:06:23,841
demand stays high, something else
tends to appear. The black market.

108
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:29,021
One of the clearest examples
of wartime mobilisation

109
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:32,931
was the United States
during the Second World War.

110
00:06:33,581 --> 00:06:37,971
Factories turned out aircraft, ships,
trucks, tanks, engines and ammunition.

111
00:06:39,161 --> 00:06:40,911
Entire production lines were reorganised.

112
00:06:42,276 --> 00:06:44,936
The important point is
that the United States did

113
00:06:45,016 --> 00:06:47,886
not simply print money and
become more productive.

114
00:06:48,836 --> 00:06:53,976
It mobilised what it already had.
People. Factories. Raw materials. Energy.

115
00:06:54,056 --> 00:06:55,826
Technology. Logistics.

116
00:06:56,096 --> 00:06:58,226
And it built more capacity as well.

117
00:07:00,976 --> 00:07:04,426
And then comes another
problem. The war is over.

118
00:07:05,255 --> 00:07:07,365
Millions of soldiers come home.

119
00:07:07,915 --> 00:07:11,065
Factories that built weapons
need something new to build.

120
00:07:11,595 --> 00:07:13,025
Military spending falls.

121
00:07:13,995 --> 00:07:17,305
People want houses, cars,
refrigerators and clothes.

122
00:07:18,295 --> 00:07:20,645
But the economy cannot
switch back overnight.

123
00:07:21,624 --> 00:07:24,254
A wartime economy has enormous inertia.

124
00:07:24,904 --> 00:07:26,954
Factories have specialised equipment.

125
00:07:27,664 --> 00:07:29,734
Workers have specialised skills.

126
00:07:30,584 --> 00:07:32,574
Governments have accumulated debt.

127
00:07:33,374 --> 00:07:35,274
Getting from war back
to peace can be almost

128
00:07:35,354 --> 00:07:37,844
as difficult as getting from peace to war.

129
00:07:40,784 --> 00:07:43,374
So what is the lesson
underneath all of it?

130
00:07:44,123 --> 00:07:48,033
Every ton of steel that goes into
a tank cannot also build a railway.

131
00:07:48,955 --> 00:07:53,225
Every worker in an ammunition
factory cannot also build a house.

132
00:07:53,851 --> 00:07:55,681
War does not remove scarcity.

133
00:07:56,591 --> 00:07:58,681
It changes where the scarce things go.

134
00:08:00,095 --> 00:08:05,295
Which is the paradox. A war economy can
be extremely efficient at one thing.

135
00:08:05,375 --> 00:08:08,865
Turning a country's resources
into military power.

136
00:08:09,582 --> 00:08:12,172
But that does not
automatically make it richer.

137
00:08:13,142 --> 00:08:17,092
A successful war economy can consume
wealth that took decades to build.

138
00:08:17,936 --> 00:08:21,606
Which is why two sentences can
both be true at the same time.

139
00:08:22,256 --> 00:08:23,606
The economy is booming.

140
00:08:24,196 --> 00:08:25,846
And people are getting poorer.

141
00:08:26,655 --> 00:08:28,105
There is no contradiction.

142
00:08:28,955 --> 00:08:30,665
The first one is about production.

143
00:08:31,495 --> 00:08:34,585
The second is about what
anybody actually gets to use.

144
00:08:35,437 --> 00:08:38,967
Because in the end, war is not a
machine for creating resources.

145
00:08:39,977 --> 00:08:41,867
It is a machine for consuming them.
